DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     An iiffaaccee node is both a netgraph node and a system networking interface.
     When an iiffaaccee node is created, a new interface appears which is accessi‐
     ble via ifconfig(8).  IIffaaccee node interfaces are named ng0, ng1, etc.
     When a node is shutdown, the corresponding interface is removed and the
     interface name becomes available for reuse by future iiffaaccee nodes; new
     nodes always take the first unused interface.  The node itself is as‐
     signed the same name as its interface, unless the name already exists, in
     which case the node remains unnamed.

     An iiffaaccee node has a single hook corresponding to each supported protocol.
     Packets transmitted via the interface flow out the corresponding proto‐
     col-specific hook.  Similarly, packets received on a hook appear on the
     interface as packets received into the corresponding protocol stack.  The
     currently supported protocols are IP and IPv6.

     An iiffaaccee node can be configured as a point-to-point interface or a broad‐
     cast interface.  The configuration can only be changed when the interface
     is down.  The default mode is point-to-point.

     IIffaaccee nodes support the Berkeley Packet Filter (BPF).

HHOOOOKKSS
     This node type supports the following hooks:

     _i_n_e_t   Transmission and reception of IP packets.

     _i_n_e_t_6  Transmission and reception of IPv6 packets.

CCOONNTTRROOLL MMEESSSSAAGGEESS
     This node type supports the generic control messages, plus the following:

     NGM_IFACE_GET_IFNAME (ggeettiiffnnaammee)
          Returns the name of the associated interface as a NUL-terminated
          ASCII string.  Normally this is the same as the name of the node.

     NGM_IFACE_GET_IFINDEX (ggeettiiffiinnddeexx)
          Returns the global index of the associated interface as a 32 bit in‐
          teger.

     NGM_IFACE_POINT2POINT (ppooiinntt22ppooiinntt)
          Set the interface to point-to-point mode.  The interface must not
          currently be up.

     NGM_IFACE_BROADCAST (bbrrooaaddccaasstt)
          Set the interface to broadcast mode.  The interface must not cur‐
          rently be up.

SSHHUUTTDDOOWWNN
     This node shuts down upon receipt of a NGM_SHUTDOWN control message.  The
     associated interface is removed and becomes available for use by future
     iiffaaccee nodes.

     Unlike most other node types, an iiffaaccee node does _n_o_t go away when all
     hooks have been disconnected; rather, and explicit NGM_SHUTDOWN control
     message is required.

AALLTTQQ SSuuppppoorrtt
     The nngg__iiffaaccee interface supports ALTQ bandwidth management feature.  How‐
     ever, nngg__iiffaaccee is a special case, since it is not a physical interface
     with limited bandwidth.  One should not turn ALTQ on nngg__iiffaaccee if the lat‐
     ter corresponds to some tunneled connection, e.g. PPPoE or PPTP.  In this
     case, ALTQ should be configured on the interface that is used to transmit
     the encapsulated packets.  In case when your graph ends up with some kind
     of serial line, either synchronous or modem, the nngg__iiffaaccee is the right
     place to turn ALTQ on.

NNeessttiinngg
     nngg__iiffaaccee supports nesting, a configuration when traffic of one nngg__iiffaaccee
     interface flows through the other.  The default maximum allowed nesting
     level is 2.  It can be changed at runtime setting sysctl(8) variable
     _n_e_t_._g_r_a_p_h_._i_f_a_c_e_._m_a_x___n_e_s_t_i_n_g to the desired level of nesting.

